From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] d3d9: DXVA2 video decoders, on the decoder core D3D11's use

The Vulkan Video decoders move from d3d11 to the dxvk core
(dxvk_video_decoder.{h,cpp}: DxvkVideoDecoder, DxvkVideoDecodeSupport,
and DxvkVideoDecodeOutput, which each API implements to put a decoded
picture into its surface); d3d11's decoder becomes the API's side of it.

d3d9 gains DXVA2's IDirectXVideoDecoderService and IDirectXVideoDecoder
on the same core. dxva2.dll asks the device for the service by a private
interface id (IID_ISgD3D9VideoDecoderService; Wine's dxva2 does, wine-sg
7849). A decoded picture goes into the program's NV12 surface's system
memory copy -- the luma rows, then the chroma at pitch x height, as
LockRect gives it, which waits for the copy -- and through DXVK's NV12
converter into what it is drawn from. 8-bit profiles only: this D3D9
makes no P010 surfaces. d3d9.videoDecode (Auto: NVIDIA's driver only),
as d3d11.videoDecode.

Bit-exact through FFmpeg's dxva2 hwaccel on NVIDIA's driver: H.264 (High,
B-frames, cropped), HEVC Main, AV1 Main 8-bit (tiles), VP9 profile 0
(tiles, segmentation).
